WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.720 --> 00:00:10.240
The Genome Browser contains tracks that we can&nbsp;
use to visualize regions of the DNA that are

00:00:10.240 --> 00:00:17.600
transcribed into RNA. This type of data can provide&nbsp;
strong supporting evidence to the hypothesis that

00:00:17.600 --> 00:00:24.720
a certain region of a genome codes for a gene.&nbsp;
Let's look into the RNA-Seq tracks section

00:00:24.720 --> 00:00:33.200
of the Genome Browser to visualize this type of&nbsp;
data. We are once again using the July 2014 (Gene)

00:00:33.200 --> 00:00:41.600
assembly of D. melanogaster and focusing on contig1. The RNA Seq Tracks section contains results

00:00:41.600 --> 00:00:48.240
from sequencing mRNAs and then mapping these&nbsp;
sequencing reads against the genome. Hence, regions

00:00:48.240 --> 00:00:54.160
with RNA-Seq read coverage usually correspond to&nbsp;
regions in the genome that are being transcribed.

00:00:55.360 --> 00:01:03.200
To visualize the RNA-Seq read coverage data, click&nbsp;
on the  RNA-Seq Coverage link under the RNA Seq

00:01:03.200 --> 00:01:08.880
Tracks section to open another window in&nbsp;
order to specify the settings for this track.

00:01:10.320 --> 00:01:16.640
Change the Display mode to "full." Change the&nbsp;
Data view scaling field to "use vertical

00:01:16.640 --> 00:01:23.440
viewing range" setting, and change the "max" field&nbsp;
under the Vertical viewing range section to 37.

00:01:24.400 --> 00:01:30.880
Unselect the Adult Males track under the&nbsp;
List subtracks section, and then click "submit."

00:01:32.000 --> 00:01:39.120
By unselecting the Adult Males option, the genome&nbsp;
browser will only show the RNA-Seq read coverage

00:01:39.120 --> 00:01:46.480
from the Adult Female sample. If we did not&nbsp;
unselect the Adult Males option, the default

00:01:46.480 --> 00:01:53.040
would be to display both the female and male&nbsp;
data as two separate subtracks on the Genome

00:01:53.040 --> 00:02:00.240
Browser. In addition to RNA-Seq coverage, it is
often also helpful to consider splice junction

00:02:00.240 --> 00:02:07.040
prediction data that are derived from the subset&nbsp;
of RNA-Seq reads that span two or more exons,

00:02:07.040 --> 00:02:13.760
known as spliced RNA-Seq reads. To visualize these&nbsp;
predictions, click on the Exon Junctions link

00:02:13.760 --> 00:02:20.000
under the RNA Seq Tracks section, use the drop&nbsp;
down menu to select "pack," and then click "submit."

00:02:20.880 --> 00:02:26.320
We can examine the splice junction predictions&nbsp;
more carefully by zooming into the five prime and

00:02:26.320 --> 00:02:34.000
three prime end of the exon. Several other tools&nbsp;
can help us analyze splice junctions. TopHat

00:02:34.000 --> 00:02:39.600
is a program that can identify splice junctions&nbsp;
without relying on a database of known junctions.

00:02:40.720 --> 00:02:46.720
TopHat first identifies potential exons by&nbsp;
mapping segments of RNA-Seq reads to the genome.

00:02:47.520 --> 00:02:54.560
because RNA-Seq reads are short sequences derived&nbsp;
from processed mature mRNA, they will often align

00:02:54.560 --> 00:03:00.800
contiguously with an exon. However, some&nbsp;
RNA-Seq reads will span multiple exons

00:03:01.360 --> 00:03:07.840
thus containing valuable information about&nbsp;
splice sites, TopHat builds a database of possible

00:03:07.840 --> 00:03:13.840
splice junctions followed by mapping the actual&nbsp;
sequencing reads against these predicted junctions.

00:03:14.960 --> 00:03:21.600
This method allows us to find novel splice sites,&nbsp;
aligns RNA-Seq reads to the genome assembly, and then

00:03:21.600 --> 00:03:28.960
analyzes the mapping results to identify the&nbsp;
splice junctions between exons. To illustrate,

00:03:28.960 --> 00:03:33.120
let's view the RNA-Seq data for&nbsp;
a different Drosophila species.

00:03:34.480 --> 00:03:40.000
Click on the Genomes link on the main menu bar&nbsp;
to return to the Genome Browser gateway page.

00:03:41.520 --> 00:03:50.480
Change the genome field to "D. biarmipes,"&nbsp;
the assembly field to "April 2013 BCM-HGSC/Dbia_2.0,"

00:03:51.280 --> 00:04:01.680
enter "tra-pa," into the search&nbsp;
terms field and then click "submit." We are now

00:04:01.680 --> 00:04:07.760
navigating to the genomic region that contains&nbsp;
the presumed tra ortholog in the Drosophila

00:04:07.760 --> 00:04:13.840
biarmipes whole genome assembly and will examine&nbsp;
the evidence tracks for the presumed ortholog.

00:04:14.960 --> 00:04:20.480
Scroll down to the RNA Seq Tracks section&nbsp;
and then change the display mode for the

00:04:20.480 --> 00:04:26.240
modENCODE TopHat Junctions track to "pack"&nbsp;
to view the individual splice junctions.

00:04:27.600 --> 00:04:33.440
After clicking "refresh," we now see a single&nbsp;
track with the collection of all TopHat

00:04:33.440 --> 00:04:39.440
splice junction predictions from the mixed&nbsp;
embryos, adult female, and adult male samples.

00:04:40.800 --> 00:04:46.080
In order to create this track, splice junction&nbsp;
predictions from the different libraries

00:04:46.080 --> 00:04:52.160
are filtered and then merged together. The&nbsp;
predictions are color-coded to indicate the

00:04:52.160 --> 00:04:59.120
number of spliced RNA reads that support the&nbsp;
predicted splice junction. Red indicates that

00:04:59.120 --> 00:05:05.680
there are more than one thousand spliced RNA-Seq&nbsp;
reads supporting the junction, followed by brown,

00:05:05.680 --> 00:05:12.880
pink, green, blue, and black in descending order,
with black corresponding to fewer than 10 reads.

00:05:14.160 --> 00:05:18.800
Several other evidence tracks are&nbsp;
available under the RNA Seq Tracks section.

00:05:19.600 --> 00:05:25.040
You may click on the track title link above&nbsp;
the display mode drop down list to learn more

00:05:25.040 --> 00:05:37.760
about how that evidence track was created and the&nbsp;
display convention used by that particular track.

